//! Error-number-to-name lookup -- the Rust behind `lib/errname.c`.
//!
//! Ported from `lib/errname.c` (6.18.49).
//!
//! Replaces: lib/errname.c
//!
//! # Every table entry is sourced from the header that defines it
//!
//! `NAMES_0`/`NAMES_512` below were generated by parsing
//! `include/uapi/asm-generic/errno-base.h`, `include/uapi/asm-generic/errno.h`,
//! and `include/linux/errno.h` for their `#define NAME VALUE` lines and
//! placing `NAME` at index `VALUE` -- not transcribed from the C's own
//! sparse designated-initializer array by hand. Where a value has two
//! macro names (`EWOULDBLOCK` == `EAGAIN`, `EDEADLOCK` == `EDEADLK`), the
//! header's first definition wins, matching which name the C's own table
//! ends up holding at that slot.
//!
//! # The leading `-` in every stored string is not decorative
//!
//! The C stores `"-EPERM"` and returns either the full string (`err < 0`)
//! or a pointer one byte in, skipping the `-` (`err > 0`) -- one static
//! string serving both call shapes via pointer arithmetic, no allocation.
//! Reproduced identically here for the same reason: there is nowhere to
//! build a second, dash-free string at runtime without an allocator.
//!
//! # The MIPS special case is dropped, not ported
//!
//! Upstream's `__errname()` has one more fallback: `IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_MIPS)
//! && err == EDQUOT` returns `"-EDQUOT"` for MIPS's out-of-range `EDQUOT`
//! (1133, arising from MIPS's sparser errno numbering -- see the C's own
//! comment). This project only ever builds x86-64, where `CONFIG_MIPS` is
//! not merely off but cannot exist, so `IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_MIPS)` is not a
//! run-time-false condition to preserve (contrast `blake2s_rs.rs`'s `DEBUG`
//! flag, which genuinely could be turned on for this same file) -- it is
//! dead in every configuration this port's Kconfig admits, and dropped
//! rather than carried as unreachable code.

use core::ffi::{c_char, c_int, CStr};

